{"type":"thinking","text":"Good corroboration:\n- `.dsh/SYNC.md:107-111` documents expected result: agent-default-model naming llmgateway and deepseek-v4.1-flash-1017; opencode-go names deepseek-v4.1-flash.\n- `fanout-run.cjs:3006-3009` model → route patch mapping.\n- `.dsh/README.md:67` says swap in opencode-go route and set OPENCODE_API_KEY.\n- Note discrepancy: tasks.md says model id at \"lines 14 and 23\" and baseURL line 9; the current actual files have model at 15/24 and baseURL at 10 for opencode-go (because the file has a 2-line header). This is a documented-vs-file line drift in a prior packet. Since my research question asks to cite each with path and line, I should cite the current actual lines from the file reads, and I can note the prior packet's stale line references as an edge case/contradiction. This is a good \"contradictory evidence\" finding: prior packet tasks.md:46 says baseURL line 9 for opencode-go, but the current file has baseURL at line 10 and model at lines 15/24. The code/file wins. This demonstrates careful research and edge case handling. However, does it confuse the answer? I can add a finding: \"Prior packet documentation cites the OpenCode Go file's baseURL at line 9 and model at lines 15/24? Wait tasks.md said lines 14 and 23. The current file actually has baseURL at line 10 and model at 15/24.\" Actually for the LLM Gateway file, tasks.md T003 says model at lines 14 and 23 — that matches the actual file (line 14 id, line 23 model). For opencode-go, T004 says model at \"lines 14 and 23\", but actual is 15 and 24 (file gained a comment line at top). And baseURL line 9 vs actual 10. So the doc is stale for opencode-go only. That's a genuine contradiction to record as an edge case rather than a finding? The persona says contradictory evidence: preserve both claims, state which is better supported, avoid marking question answered unless resolved. Here it IS resolved: the current file is authoritative for what the patch configures now; the doc line numbers are stale. The patch content claims (provider/model) agree; only line numbers drifted. So not a real contradiction of facts about the route configuration, just stale citations. I'll record it under Edge Cases (contradictory evidence, resolved in favor of the current file) and maybe as a ruled-out line? Better: Edge Cases section with sources. Not counted as a finding (to keep findingsCount 6). Hmm, the output contract wants findingsCount = number of findings enumerated. Edge Cases don't count as findings. Fine.\n\nShould I include the mapping (fanout-run) as finding 6? The research topic only asks what the two patches configure; the mapping adds context of which is selected. It's a useful 6th finding but arguably out of the core ask. The instruction says \"Which endpoint, model id and reasoning effort do the two dsh route patches configure? Cite each with its path and line.\" A direct answer needs per patch: endpoint, model id, reasoning effort = 6 data points, which I've structured as 5-6 findings. Let me restructure cleanly:\n\nFinding 1: LLM Gateway endpoint: provider `llmgateway`, baseURL `https://api.llmgateway.io/v1`, API `openai-completions`, key env `LLMGATEWAY_API_KEY` — lines 5-9.\nFinding 2: LLM Gateway model: `deepseek-v4.1-flash-1017` (line 14), reasoning efforts off/high/max (16-19), and default dispatch pinned to that provider/model at `reasoningEffort: max` (20-24).\nFinding 3: OpenCode Go endpoint: provider `opencode-go`, baseURL `https://opencode.ai/zen/go/v1`, API `openai-completions`, key env `OPENCODE_API_KEY` — lines 5-10; file header says endpoint unverified until a keyed run (line 2).\nFinding 4: OpenCode Go model: `deepseek-v4.1-flash` (line 15), reasoning efforts off/high/max (17-20), default dispatch pinned to that provider/model at `reasoningEffort: max` (21-25).\nFinding 5: Selection rule: `deepseek-v4.1-flash-1017` → llmgateway patch, else → opencode-go patch (fanout-run.cjs:3006-3009), and this lineage's invocation metadata records model `deepseek-v4.1-flash-1017` with reasoningEffort `max` (invocation-metadata.json:1), so the LLM Gateway patch is the active one here.\nFinding 6: Independent corroboration: `.dsh/SYNC.md:111` documents the expected composed defaults (`llmgateway` + `deepseek-v4.1-flash-1017`; `opencode-go` + `deepseek-v4.1-flash`), matching the patches; `.dsh/README.md:67` names the key env for the OpenCode Go swap (`OPENCODE_API_KEY`). — This is a partially new/duplicate finding? It corroborates, so it's a \"partially new\" (0.5) perhaps. But since it's a first iteration, all evidence is new to the packet. Honesty: findings 1-4 are fully new; finding 5 partially new (derived mapping, adds context); finding 6 partially new (corroboration, no new configuration fact). That would give ratio = (4 + 0.5 + 0.5)/6 = 0.833. Hmm, \"fully new findings count as 1.0; partially new count 0.5\". Finding 5 is a new fact (selection rule) fully new. Finding 6 is corroboration but adds doc references and a stale-line check, partially new. Ratio = (5 + 0.5)/6 = 0.917. Let me decide: 5 findings fully new, 1 partially new → (5 + 0.5)/6 = 0.9167 → 0.92. That seems honest. Or count finding 6 as fully new because the corroboration sources weren't consulted before. \"New information\" relative to accumulated research knowledge (empty packet) — all citations are new. But finding 6 adds no new configuration data, only corroboration. I'd call it partially new. 0.92.\n\nHmm, simplicity bonus +0.10? The synthesis \"provides a cleaner model without new external evidence\" — not applicable, we have new evidence. Don't add.\n\nFindingsCount 6, newInfoRatio 0.92.\n\nNow let me also check `.dsh/SYNC.md` around lines 100-115 to cite precisely, and `.dsh/README.md` line 61-67. Let me read those spans. Also maybe the `.dsh/patches` files could change between read and write? No.\n\nLet me also verify current line numbers with `nl -ba`? The read tool gave numbered content; that's enough. But to be strict about exact lines, I have them.\n\nNext: phase_init. Create config etc. But first, the config: I need to include fields the reducer expects. Let me inspect reduce-state.cjs a bit for required config fields (progressiveSynthesis, resource_map.emit, etc.). Also how it finds spec folder. Let me read the top ~120 lines and grep for config fields."}
