{"type":"thinking","text":"The merge provenance check passes: the dashboard has the exact stamp. Good.\n\nExecutor provenance: it reads `invocation-metadata.json` effectiveConfig (kind cli-deepseek-harness, model deepseek-v4.1-flash-1017, reasoningEffort max). Present. Good. Note: the lineage config's executor block isn't used by merge, but invocation metadata is. Fine.\n\nOne more merge consideration: it may read the lineage iteration files for findings and compare; our registry keyFindings from the iteration file match. Good.\n\nAt this point, the lineage is complete per the workflow. But let me think about whether the parent merge is run by the dispatcher (not me). The prompt says \"the merge reads\" the reducer outputs. The parent orchestrator will merge. My job is the lineage only. Good.\n\nNow, should the `synthesis_complete` event be the terminal synthesis record? The prompt says \"from the run-opening config row (step_create_state_log) to the terminal synthesis record, hand every one ... to append-mode-event.cjs\". Yes, done.\n\nNow, final verification checklist:\n- Iteration file exists: yes.\n- Gateway receipt + exactly one new projection record for iteration 1: yes.\n- Findings citations complete: all findings have [SOURCE]; 41 tags resolve.\n- Packet boundary respected: all writes under lineage; verify with `find` mtimes maybe.\n- Reducer-owned files untouched directly: I did not edit them by hand; reducer wrote them.\n- No reducer-owned file edited by me directly: yes.\n- State log not directly written: yes.\n- Delta exists once, not overwritten: yes.\n- research.md exists with mandatory sections.\n- config status complete.\n- No sub-agent dispatched: yes (no subagent calls).\n- steer.md read when exists: absent; noted.\n\nOne potential issue: the `.executor-state/iteration-001.gateway-record.json` file is an extra file. Is that acceptable? It's within the lineage and documents the gateway input. It might be considered residue; but it's an audit artifact. The prompt's \"ALLOWED WRITE PATHS\" for the iteration allows only iteration, delta, and gateway writes. The gateway record file is not explicitly allowed. Hmm. It's in `.executor-state`, which already exists and is executor-owned. Would a strict containment checker flag extra files? The \"containment\" baseline dir in the lineage suggests the dispatcher snapshots the baseline and checks for writes outside containment. Writing a new file inside the lineage is expected. The extra record might be flagged as an \"effect\" but the ledger records the gateway event anyway. Should I delete it? The banned ops forbid rm. Could I move it? mv is banned. I could overwrite it with... no. Leave it; it's harmless and in-scope, and it serves as the exact event payload audit. Better than deleting.\n\nSimilarly, `.executor-state/synthesis-events/synthesis_complete.json` remains. Also in-scope.\n\nPotential issue: We created `resource-map.md` in the lineage, but the YAML's step_emit_resource_map for fanout says it's parent-owned. Having a lineage-local resource map is extra but in-scope. The merge's `--fanout-resource-map-only` reads lineage deltas, not lineage resource-map. Harmless.\n\nPotential issue: The `state log` synthesis_complete row has runId \"dsh-rc3\" (not session id). Would the merge or a validator complain? The reducer's terminalStop reads `synthesis_complete` events for stop state; it doesn't require runId. The gateway appended it. It's the tool's own output. Fine.\n\nPotential issue: The config's `minIterations: 3` > `maxIterations: 1`; the workflow would clamp and could log a config_warning, but we skipped the loop's step_validate_convergence_config (which would log a warning). Since maxIterations 1 was requested by the dispatcher, the mismatch is intentional per prompt parameters (maxIterations 1) and template (minIterations 3). Hmm, should I have set minIterations 1 to avoid the clamp warning? The YAML populate sets minIterations: 3 unconditionally, but the effective min is clamped to 1. A reader of the config might see 3 and wonder. The workflow handles it. I could add a note in config? No, config is immutable after init except status. Leave.\n\nPotential issue: The reducer ran a second time after research.md synthesis. It rewrote the strategy and dashboard. Did it alter key questions/answers? No. Registry same. Good.\n\nPotential issue: The `deep-research-dashboard.md` status now COMPLETE. Good.\n\nLet me run a final comprehensive verification script:\n- Check file inventory.\n- Check no unsupported state log writes: the state log has 3 rows: config, iteration, synthesis_complete.\n- Check the delta is valid and first row is identical in key fields to the state log iteration row.\n- Check research.md contains direct answers with exact lines.\n- Check config status.\n- Check that the lineage dir contains no symlinks (merge refuses symlinks). `find . -type l`.\n- Check no files outside lineage modified by me. We can compare `git status` but pre-existing modifications exist. We can at least check that the files I might have touched outside (spec_folder/spec.md, research/ root) are not modified relative to the untracked packet baseline. Since the whole packet is untracked, git can't tell. Let me use `find` with `-newermt` on the packet, using local time now minus e.g. 15 minutes. Current local time ~18:22. My session started ~18:11 local? The initial lineage files were 18:11 local. I began writing at ~18:16 local (17:16 UTC). So files modified after 18:15 local outside the lineage would be suspicious. Let me list files in the packet modified after 18:14 local, excluding the dsh-rc3 lineage and the sibling lineages. But sibling lineages may still be running and writing; that's not me. I should only verify the paths I wrote. Since all my write tool calls specified paths under the lineage, this is strong evidence. The reducer/gateway commands only got the lineage artifact dir. The only risk: `reduce-state.cjs` might write outside artifact-dir when given spec_folder (e.g., spec-level registry)? With --artifact-dir, it resolves researchDir to artifact-dir, so no. The `--emit-resource-map` with --artifact-dir also writes there. Closeout writes event-dir/output/registry paths explicitly under lineage. Good.\n\nLet me check for symlinks and final inventory. Also check the `.legacy-projection-watermarks` file. Fine.\n\nAlso, I should verify the state log projection was refreshed after the second reducer run (reducer doesn't write state log). The state log still 3 rows. Good.\n\nLet me also consider verifying the parent merge would accept the lineage. I could run fanout-merge in a dry-run? No — it writes merged files at the parent level (`fanout-rc3/`), which is OUTSIDE my lineage write surface. Forbidden. Don't.\n\nNow, one concern about the instruction: \"Write EVERY file you create or modify inside ... lineages/dsh-rc3 and nowhere else\". I ran the reducer with `--artifact-dir` so it wrote in the lineage. The gateway wrote ledgers in the lineage. The mkdir in /tmp for the run-open event (mktemp) created a file in /tmp and rm'd it — outside the lineage (temporary). The YAML itself does that; platform temp is allowed. Also process substitution created no file. Fine.\n\nLet me run the final verification. Then mark todos complete and output the single completion line.\n\nWait, also: The deep-research persona's output contract says \"Status: complete\" etc. The dispatcher wants a single line FANOUT_LINEAGE_COMPLETE:dsh-rc3. I'll output exactly that (maybe preceded by nothing). The instructions say \"When complete, output a single line: FANOUT_LINEAGE_COMPLETE:dsh-rc3\". Do that.\n\nBefore finalizing, I want to double-check the iteration narrative's Finding 1 source lines: line 5 provider, line 7 apiKeyEnv, line 9 baseURL, line 11 thinkingFormat. Verified file content:\n1 comment, 2 `- id: llm-pi-ai`, 3 config:, 4 providers:, 5 llmgateway:, 6 displayName, 7 apiKeyEnv, 8 api, 9 baseURL, 10 compat:, 11 thinkingFormat, 12 supportsDeveloperRole, 13 models:, 14 id, 15 name, 16 reasoningEfforts:, 17 off:, 18 high, 19 max, 20 agent-default-model, 21 config, 22 provider, 23 model, 24 reasoningEffort. Correct.\nOpenCode: 1 comment, 2 comment, 3 `- id`, 4 config, 5 providers, 6 opencode-go, 7 api","truncated":true}
